# `using` a unit module must not capture unrelated conversions.
#
# Units.SI defines its formatting as TOP-LEVEL functions whose first parameter
# is annotated `Measure` (`let to(measure: Measure, String, in: SIPrefix)`),
# which is what makes `using` the boundary for reaching them. But a bare-name
# call cannot consult a parameter annotation: the import simply rebound `to`,
# so EVERY conversion in the program went to Units.SI and answered None —
# `42.to(String)`, `42.to(Integer)`, `"7".to(Integer)` — and `undef` on BEAM,
# where the same capture happened through module-call rewriting and through a
# trait dictionary aimed at `Kex.Units.SI:showValue/1`, a function that module
# does not define.
#
# Both halves are needed, and each hides the other: with only the walker fix
# this program still dies on BEAM, and with only the lowering fix the walker
# still prints None three times.
using Units.SI

main do
  # The conversions the import used to swallow.
  IO.printLine("${42.to(String)} ${42.to(Integer)} ${"7".to(Integer)}")

  # ...while the unit module's own formatting still works, positionally and
  # by name. This is what `using Units.SI` is FOR, so a fix that restores the
  # conversions by breaking these is not a fix.
  IO.printLine("${3.kilo.watt.to(String)}")
  IO.printLine("${3.kilo.watt.to(String, in: Mega)}")
end
